From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] pwm: add a driver for the A733 (sun60iw2) PWM controllers

Mainline 6.18.36 has exactly one Allwinner PWM driver, pwm-sun4i.c, and it
cannot drive this block. Checked rather than assumed: drivers/pwm/ contains
pwm-sun4i.c and pwm-sunplus.c and nothing else with an "allwinner," compatible,
Documentation/devicetree/bindings/pwm/ has one Allwinner file, and there is no
pwm-sun20i*.c anywhere in the tree.

Register by register against A733 UM V0.92 chapter 18.7 (register list in 18.7.7):

  A733 UM 18.7.7            offset   pwm-sun4i.c expects there
  PIER, IRQ enable          0x0000   PWM_CTRL_REG
  PISR, IRQ status          0x0004   channel 0 period
  PCCR01..89, clock cfg     0x0020   channel 3 period
  PCGR, clock gating        0x0040   -
  PER, channel enable       0x0080   -            (sun4i: CTRL bit 4)
  PCR(N), control           0x0100 + N * 0x0040   -
  PPR(N), period            0x0104 + N * 0x0040   -

pwm-sun4i's first write would land in an interrupt-enable register, so this is a
new driver, not a compatible string.

The value this patch exists to get right is the per-channel stride. The
long-pending upstream series for this same second-generation block ("pwm: Add
Allwinner's D1/T113-S3/R329 SoCs PWM support", v12 of 2025-04-27, still not
merged as of 6.18.36) names the A733 in its own cover letter as one of the parts
that carry it, and it matches this SoC exactly across the whole global register
block: PCCR at 0x20 + pair * 4 with CLK_SRC in bits 8:7 and DIV_M in bits 3:0,
PCGR at 0x40 with gating in bit N and bypass in bit N+16, PER at 0x80 with
enable in bit N, PCR.ACT_STA at bit 8, PCR.PRESCAL_K in bits 7:0, PPR split
31:16 / 15:0. Every one of those was re-derived here from the A733 UM and every
one agrees.

It then places the per-channel registers at 0x100 + N * 0x20, and on the A733
the stride is 0x40. The UM says so twice and unambiguously: the register list
and every per-channel register description
both read "0x0100+0x00xx+N*0x0040 (N= 0~9)". The reason is visible in
the register list -- this part carries three capture-cycle-counter registers per
channel (CCCSR, CCCNR, CCCR at 0x1C, 0x20, 0x24) that the D1 does not, so a
channel block reaches 0x24 and cannot fit in 0x20.

Inherited unchecked, that value is the exact failure this tree has a name for: it
compiles, channel 0 works, and every other channel writes into its neighbour.
Channel 1's control register would go to 0x120, which on the A733 is channel 0's
CCCNR. Channel 9 -- the one this board's fan hangs off -- would be written at
0x220, inside channel 4. The fan would never turn and no register would report an
error.

What the driver does and does not model:

- Cycle mode only. Pulse mode (PCR.PWM_MODE + PUL_NUM + PUL_START), the dead
  zone generator, group mode and capture are all left at their reset values.
- The clock bypass bit (PCGR bit N+16, which routes the divided source clock
  straight to the pin instead of through the counter) is cleared on every apply
  and never set. get_state warns once if it finds it set, rather than reporting a
  duty cycle that is not what the pin is doing.
- PCCR is per channel PAIR -- source and the /1../256 divider are shared between
  channels 2n and 2n+1, only PRESCAL_K and the counters are per channel. A
  request that would need a different PCCR while the paired channel is enabled
  is refused with -EBUSY and a dev_err naming both channels, rather than served
  by silently reprogramming somebody else's clock.
- Both sources are offered to every request and the one leaving the most counter
  steps in the period wins, because that count is the duty-cycle resolution the
  consumer gets. Rates come from clk_get_rate(), never from a constant: the
  driver has no opinion about 24 versus 26 MHz and the device tree is where that
  has to be right.
- Rounding is down at every step, as the PWM API requires. One corner is
  documented in the code: ACT_CYCLE is 16 bit while ENTIRE_CYCLE counts to
  65536, so a period using the very last counter step tops out at 65535/65536
  rather than 100 %.

Probe deliberately enables nothing. It maps the registers, takes the three
clocks and de-asserts the reset, and writes no PER, PCGR or PCR bit. PER, PCGR
and every PCR are 0 out of reset (UM 18.7.8), so a controller that no consumer
has requested a channel from leaves its pins alone -- which is what lets the SoC
device tree carry this node for every A733 board, including one that muxes a PWM
pin to something else.

Applies clean and builds (compile-check gate). NOT hardware-proven. What would
prove it, in order: PWM0/PWM1 appear as pwmchips; a channel programmed to a
known period shows that period on a scope or on the fan; and the value this
patch is really about -- a channel other than 0 does something, i.e. channel 9
moves and channels 4 and 8 do not.

One thing this patch cannot answer and which the UM does not either: what level
a channel's pin sits at while the channel is disabled. UM 18.7.8.18 defines
PER.PWMn_EN as "0: PWM disable" and says nothing about the output. That matters
for a board that muxes the pin before a consumer drives it, and it has to be
measured, not deduced.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/pwm/pwm-sun60i-a733.c
@@ -0,0 +1,523 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+/*
+ * Allwinner A733 (sun60iw2) PWM controller driver.
+ *
+ * Copyright (c) 2026 DockSeed A733 Debian mainline track
+ *
+ * The part has three of these blocks -- PWM0 @ 0x02527000, PWM1 @ 0x02528000
+ * and S_PWM @ 0x07023000, ten channels each (A733 UM V0.92 18.7.7).
+ * This driver claims the first two. S_PWM is deliberately left
+ * out: its register file is documented as identical, but its clock sources are
+ * not (DCXO / RTC_32K / RC16M / SYS_CLK24M against SYS_CLK24M / APB0),
+ * so its PCCR source field is a different table that has not
+ * been read here. Claiming it would be a guess.
+ *
+ * Why this is not pwm-sun4i, and not the sun20i-d1 layout either. Every offset
+ * below is from A733 UM chapter 18.7, and the two divergences are the point of
+ * this file:
+ *
+ *   register              sun4i (pwm-sun4i.c)   A733 UM 18.7.7
+ *   0x0000                PWM_CTRL_REG          PIER, IRQ enable
+ *   0x0004                channel 0 period      PISR, IRQ status
+ *   channel enable        CTRL bit 4            PER 0x0080 bit N
+ *   channel registers     0x0004 + ch * 0x04    0x0100 + ch * 0x0040
+ *
+ * so pwm-sun4i cannot drive this block at all: its very first write would land
+ * in an interrupt-enable register.
+ *
+ * The second divergence is the one that looks harmless and is not. The
+ * long-pending upstream series for this same second-generation block
+ * ("pwm: Add Allwinner's D1/T113-S3/R329 SoCs PWM support", v12 2025-04-27, not
+ * in 6.18.36) matches this part register for register in the whole global
+ * block -- PCCR at 0x20 + pair * 4 with CLK_SRC in bits 8:7 and DIV_M in 3:0,
+ * PCGR at 0x40 with gating in bit N and bypass in bit N+16, PER at 0x80 -- and
+ * then places the per-channel block at 0x100 + ch * 0x20. On the A733 the
+ * stride is 0x40 (the A733 user manual's register list and descriptions both spell out
+ * "0x0100+0x0000+N*0x0040"), because this part carries three capture cycle
+ * counter registers per channel that the D1 does not, reaching 0x24 inside the
+ * channel block. At stride 0x20 channel 1's control register would be written
+ * at 0x120, which on the A733 is channel 0's CCCNR. Channel 0 would work,
+ * every other channel would silently write into a neighbour, and the fan on
+ * channel 9 would never turn.
+ *
+ * The pair sharing is real and is not modelled away: PCCR is one register per
+ * channel pair (PCCR01, PCCR23, ... PCCR89), so the clock source and the /1../256
+ * divider are shared between channels 2n and 2n+1. Only the pre-scaler
+ * (PCR.PRESCAL_K) and the counters are per channel. A request that would need a
+ * different PCCR while the paired channel is enabled is refused with -EBUSY
+ * rather than served by reprogramming somebody else's clock.
+ *
+ * Not modelled: pulse mode (PCR.PWM_MODE, always left at cycle mode), the dead
+ * zone generator, group mode, and capture. The clock bypass bit (PCGR bit
+ * N+16, which routes the divided source clock straight to the pin instead of
+ * the counter) is cleared on every apply and never set.
+ */
+
+#include <linux/bitfield.h>
+#include <linux/bitops.h>
+#include <linux/clk.h>
+#include <linux/err.h>
+#include <linux/io.h>
+#include <linux/iopoll.h>
+#include <linux/math64.h>
+#include <linux/minmax.h>
+#include <linux/mod_devicetable.h>
+#include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/pwm.h>
+#include <linux/reset.h>
+#include <linux/time.h>
+
+/* UM 18.7.1: ten channels per controller. */
+#define A733_PWM_NPWM			10
+
+/* PCCR01..PCCR89, one register per channel pair (UM 18.7.8.7). */
+#define A733_PWM_PCCR(pair)		(0x0020 + (pair) * 0x4)
+#define A733_PWM_PCCR_CLK_SRC		GENMASK(8, 7)
+#define A733_PWM_PCCR_DIV_M		GENMASK(3, 0)
+/* DIV_M encodes 2^M: 0000 = /1 ... 1000 = /256, "others: /". */
+#define A733_PWM_DIV_M_MAX		8
+
+/* PCGR, whole-controller clock gating (UM 18.7.8.12). */
+#define A733_PWM_PCGR			0x0040
+#define A733_PWM_PCGR_BYPASS(ch)	BIT((ch) + 16)
+#define A733_PWM_PCGR_GATING(ch)	BIT(ch)
+
+/* PER, output enable (UM 18.7.8.18). */
+#define A733_PWM_PER			0x0080
+#define A733_PWM_PER_EN(ch)		BIT(ch)
+
+/*
+ * Per-channel block. The stride is the value this SoC differs on, so it is
+ * spelled out rather than folded into the register macros (UM 18.7.7 register
+ * list, and each per-channel register description from the PWM channel
+ * control register onwards: "0x0100+0x00xx+N*0x0040 (N= 0~9)").
+ */
+#define A733_PWM_CH_BASE		0x0100
+#define A733_PWM_CH_STRIDE		0x0040
+
+/* PCR, per-channel control (UM 18.7.8.25). */
+#define A733_PWM_PCR(ch)		(A733_PWM_CH_BASE + (ch) * A733_PWM_CH_STRIDE)
+#define A733_PWM_PCR_PERIOD_RDY		BIT(11)
+#define A733_PWM_PCR_MODE_PULSE		BIT(9)
+#define A733_PWM_PCR_ACT_STA		BIT(8)
+#define A733_PWM_PCR_PRESCAL_K		GENMASK(7, 0)
+/* The pre-scale divisor is K + 1 with K = PRESCAL_K = 0..255. */
+#define A733_PWM_PRESCAL_MAX		256
+
+/* PPR, per-channel period (UM 18.7.8.26). */
+#define A733_PWM_PPR(ch)		(A733_PWM_PCR(ch) + 0x4)
+#define A733_PWM_PPR_ENTIRE_CYCLE	GENMASK(31, 16)
+#define A733_PWM_PPR_ACT_CYCLE		GENMASK(15, 0)
+/* ENTIRE_CYCLE gives a period of N + 1 cycles; ACT_CYCLE gives N cycles. */
+#define A733_PWM_ENTIRE_MAX		65536
+#define A733_PWM_ACT_MAX		65535
+
+/*
+ * Bounds for the PWM_PERIOD_RDY handshake. The bit clears when the shadowed
+ * period register has been taken over, i.e. at the end of the period that is
+ * currently running, so the wait is bounded by that period and not by a
+ * constant. A stopped channel clears it at once (reset value 0 in the
+ * A733 user manual).
+ */
+#define A733_PWM_RDY_MIN_TIMEOUT_US	1000
+#define A733_PWM_RDY_MAX_TIMEOUT_US	2000000
+
+/* PCCR.CLK_SRC: 00 = SYS_CLK24M, 01 = APB0, "Others: /". */
+enum a733_pwm_clk_src {
+	A733_PWM_SRC_HOSC = 0,
+	A733_PWM_SRC_APB0 = 1,
+	A733_PWM_SRC_NUM
+};
+
+/*
+ * "hosc" is the SYS_CLK24M net -- PLL_REF, fixed at 24 MHz (A733 user manual
+ * V0.92), NOT the 26 MHz DCXO. The driver never assumes either number, it
+ * asks the clock framework; the device tree is where that distinction has to be
+ * got right.
+ */
+static const char *const a733_pwm_clk_names[A733_PWM_SRC_NUM] = {
+	[A733_PWM_SRC_HOSC] = "hosc",
+	[A733_PWM_SRC_APB0] = "apb",
+};
+
+struct a733_pwm {
+	void __iomem *base;
+	struct clk *src[A733_PWM_SRC_NUM];
+};
+
+/* One channel's programming, in hardware units. */
+struct a733_pwm_cfg {
+	unsigned int src;	/* enum a733_pwm_clk_src */
+	unsigned int div_m;	/* PCCR.DIV_M, divider is 1 << div_m */
+	unsigned int prescal;	/* PCR.PRESCAL_K + 1, i.e. 1..256 */
+	unsigned int entire;	/* PPR.ENTIRE_CYCLE + 1, i.e. 1..65536 */
+	unsigned int act;	/* PPR.ACT_CYCLE, i.e. 0..65535 */
+};
+
+static inline struct a733_pwm *to_a733_pwm(struct pwm_chip *chip)
+{
+	return pwmchip_get_drvdata(chip);
+}
+
+static unsigned long a733_pwm_src_rate(struct a733_pwm *pc,
+				       const struct a733_pwm_cfg *cfg)
+{
+	return clk_get_rate(pc->src[cfg->src]);
+}
+
+/* Total division from the source clock to one counter tick. */
+static u64 a733_pwm_cfg_div(const struct a733_pwm_cfg *cfg)
+{
+	return (u64)cfg->prescal << cfg->div_m;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Turn a requested period/duty into register values, rounding DOWN at every
+ * step: the PWM API requires that neither the applied period nor the applied
+ * duty cycle exceeds what the caller asked for.
+ */
+static int a733_pwm_calc(unsigned long rate, u64 period_ns, u64 duty_ns,
+			 struct a733_pwm_cfg *cfg)
+{
+	u64 cycles, div_needed, best_div = 0, act;
+	unsigned int m;
+
+	cycles = mul_u64_u64_div_u64(period_ns, rate, NSEC_PER_SEC);
+	if (!cycles)
+		return -EINVAL;		/* shorter than one source clock tick */
+
+	/* The counter is 16 bit, so anything above 65536 ticks needs dividing. */
+	div_needed = DIV64_U64_ROUND_UP(cycles, A733_PWM_ENTIRE_MAX);
+	if (div_needed > ((u64)A733_PWM_PRESCAL_MAX << A733_PWM_DIV_M_MAX))
+		return -EINVAL;		/* longer than the divider chain reaches */
+
+	/*
+	 * Two dividers in series: PCCR.DIV_M is a power of two up to 256,
+	 * PCR.PRESCAL_K is 1..256 and is the only one of the pair that is per
+	 * channel. Pick the smallest product that still fits the counter,
+	 * because a smaller product means more counter ticks per period and so
+	 * finer duty-cycle resolution.
+	 */
+	for (m = 0; m <= A733_PWM_DIV_M_MAX; m++) {
+		u64 k = DIV64_U64_ROUND_UP(div_needed, 1ULL << m);
+		u64 div;
+
+		if (k > A733_PWM_PRESCAL_MAX)
+			continue;
+
+		div = k << m;
+		if (!best_div || div < best_div) {
+			best_div = div;
+			cfg->div_m = m;
+			cfg->prescal = k;
+		}
+	}
+	if (!best_div)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	cycles = div64_u64(cycles, best_div);
+	if (!cycles || cycles > A733_PWM_ENTIRE_MAX)
+		return -EINVAL;
+	cfg->entire = cycles;
+
+	act = div64_u64(mul_u64_u64_div_u64(duty_ns, rate, NSEC_PER_SEC),
+			best_div);
+	act = min_t(u64, act, cfg->entire);
+	/*
+	 * ACT_CYCLE is 16 bit while ENTIRE_CYCLE counts to 65536, so a period
+	 * that uses the very last counter step cannot express 100 %. It gives
+	 * 65535/65536 instead, which is a round-down and therefore allowed.
+	 */
+	cfg->act = min_t(u64, act, A733_PWM_ACT_MAX);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/* Read back what a channel is actually programmed to. */
+static int a733_pwm_read_cfg(struct a733_pwm *pc, unsigned int ch,
+			     struct a733_pwm_cfg *cfg)
+{
+	u32 pccr, pcr, ppr;
+
+	pccr = readl(pc->base + A733_PWM_PCCR(ch / 2));
+	pcr = readl(pc->base + A733_PWM_PCR(ch));
+	ppr = readl(pc->base + A733_PWM_PPR(ch));
+
+	cfg->src = FIELD_GET(A733_PWM_PCCR_CLK_SRC, pccr);
+	if (cfg->src >= A733_PWM_SRC_NUM)
+		return -EINVAL;		/* UM: "Others: /" -- undefined */
+
+	cfg->div_m = FIELD_GET(A733_PWM_PCCR_DIV_M, pccr);
+	if (cfg->div_m > A733_PWM_DIV_M_MAX)
+		return -EINVAL;		/* UM: "others: /" -- undefined */
+
+	cfg->prescal = FIELD_GET(A733_PWM_PCR_PRESCAL_K, pcr) + 1;
+	cfg->entire = FIELD_GET(A733_PWM_PPR_ENTIRE_CYCLE, ppr) + 1;
+	cfg->act = FIELD_GET(A733_PWM_PPR_ACT_CYCLE, ppr);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static u64 a733_pwm_cycles_to_ns(unsigned long rate,
+				 const struct a733_pwm_cfg *cfg,
+				 unsigned int cycles)
+{
+	/*
+	 * Bounded: 65536 counter steps * 65536 division * 1e9 is 4.3e18, which
+	 * still fits an u64.
+	 */
+	return DIV_ROUND_UP_ULL((u64)cycles * a733_pwm_cfg_div(cfg) * NSEC_PER_SEC,
+				rate);
+}
+
+static bool a733_pwm_is_running(struct a733_pwm *pc, unsigned int ch)
+{
+	return (readl(pc->base + A733_PWM_PER) & A733_PWM_PER_EN(ch)) &&
+	       (readl(pc->base + A733_PWM_PCGR) & A733_PWM_PCGR_GATING(ch));
+}
+
+static int a733_pwm_wait_period_ready(struct pwm_chip *chip, unsigned int ch)
+{
+	struct a733_pwm *pc = to_a733_pwm(chip);
+	struct a733_pwm_cfg cur;
+	unsigned long rate;
+	u64 timeout_us = A733_PWM_RDY_MIN_TIMEOUT_US;
+	u32 pcr;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (!a733_pwm_read_cfg(pc, ch, &cur)) {
+		rate = a733_pwm_src_rate(pc, &cur);
+		if (rate) {
+			u64 period = a733_pwm_cycles_to_ns(rate, &cur, cur.entire);
+
+			timeout_us = clamp_t(u64,
+					     DIV_ROUND_UP_ULL(period * 2, NSEC_PER_USEC),
+					     A733_PWM_RDY_MIN_TIMEOUT_US,
+					     A733_PWM_RDY_MAX_TIMEOUT_US);
+		}
+	}
+
+	ret = readl_poll_timeout(pc->base + A733_PWM_PCR(ch), pcr,
+				 !(pcr & A733_PWM_PCR_PERIOD_RDY),
+				 10, timeout_us);
+	if (ret)
+		dev_err(pwmchip_parent(chip),
+			"ch%u: period register stayed busy for %lluus\n",
+			ch, timeout_us);
+
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static void a733_pwm_disable(struct a733_pwm *pc, unsigned int ch)
+{
+	u32 val;
+
+	val = readl(pc->base + A733_PWM_PER);
+	writel(val & ~A733_PWM_PER_EN(ch), pc->base + A733_PWM_PER);
+
+	val = readl(pc->base + A733_PWM_PCGR);
+	writel(val & ~A733_PWM_PCGR_GATING(ch), pc->base + A733_PWM_PCGR);
+}
+
+static int a733_pwm_apply(struct pwm_chip *chip, struct pwm_device *pwm,
+			  const struct pwm_state *state)
+{
+	struct a733_pwm *pc = to_a733_pwm(chip);
+	unsigned int ch = pwm->hwpwm, pair = ch / 2, src;
+	struct a733_pwm_cfg cfg = {}, cand;
+	bool found = false;
+	u32 pccr, val;
+	int ret;
+
+	if (!state->enabled) {
+		a733_pwm_disable(pc, ch);
+		return 0;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Both sources can serve most requests; take whichever leaves the most
+	 * counter steps in the period, since that is the duty-cycle resolution
+	 * the consumer gets.
+	 */
+	for (src = 0; src < A733_PWM_SRC_NUM; src++) {
+		unsigned long rate = clk_get_rate(pc->src[src]);
+
+		if (!rate)
+			continue;
+		if (a733_pwm_calc(rate, state->period, state->duty_cycle, &cand))
+			continue;
+
+		cand.src = src;
+		if (!found || cand.entire > cfg.entire) {
+			cfg = cand;
+			found = true;
+		}
+	}
+	if (!found)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	/*
+	 * PCCR is per channel PAIR. Reprogramming it for this channel would
+	 * change the paired channel's clock underneath it, so refuse instead --
+	 * loudly, because "the fan is quiet" is not a diagnosis.
+	 */
+	pccr = FIELD_PREP(A733_PWM_PCCR_CLK_SRC, cfg.src) |
+	       FIELD_PREP(A733_PWM_PCCR_DIV_M, cfg.div_m);
+	val = readl(pc->base + A733_PWM_PCCR(pair));
+	if ((val & (A733_PWM_PCCR_CLK_SRC | A733_PWM_PCCR_DIV_M)) != pccr) {
+		if (readl(pc->base + A733_PWM_PER) & A733_PWM_PER_EN(ch ^ 1)) {
+			dev_err(pwmchip_parent(chip),
+				"ch%u needs PCCR%u%u changed while ch%u is running\n",
+				ch, pair * 2, pair * 2 + 1, ch ^ 1);
+			return -EBUSY;
+		}
+		writel(pccr, pc->base + A733_PWM_PCCR(pair));
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * The counter needs its clock before the period handshake can complete,
+	 * and bypass (raw clock straight to the pin) is not a mode this driver
+	 * offers, so clear it whatever firmware left behind.
+	 */
+	val = readl(pc->base + A733_PWM_PCGR);
+	val &= ~A733_PWM_PCGR_BYPASS(ch);
+	val |= A733_PWM_PCGR_GATING(ch);
+	writel(val, pc->base + A733_PWM_PCGR);
+
+	/*
+	 * PWM_ACT_STA says which level the active cycles drive: 1 = high, so
+	 * that is PWM_POLARITY_NORMAL. Writing PCR also clears PWM_MODE, which
+	 * is what we want -- cycle mode, not a finite pulse train.
+	 */
+	val = FIELD_PREP(A733_PWM_PCR_PRESCAL_K, cfg.prescal - 1);
+	if (state->polarity == PWM_POLARITY_NORMAL)
+		val |= A733_PWM_PCR_ACT_STA;
+	writel(val, pc->base + A733_PWM_PCR(ch));
+
+	ret = a733_pwm_wait_period_ready(chip, ch);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	writel(FIELD_PREP(A733_PWM_PPR_ENTIRE_CYCLE, cfg.entire - 1) |
+	       FIELD_PREP(A733_PWM_PPR_ACT_CYCLE, cfg.act),
+	       pc->base + A733_PWM_PPR(ch));
+
+	val = readl(pc->base + A733_PWM_PER);
+	writel(val | A733_PWM_PER_EN(ch), pc->base + A733_PWM_PER);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int a733_pwm_get_state(struct pwm_chip *chip, struct pwm_device *pwm,
+			      struct pwm_state *state)
+{
+	struct a733_pwm *pc = to_a733_pwm(chip);
+	unsigned int ch = pwm->hwpwm;
+	struct a733_pwm_cfg cfg;
+	unsigned long rate;
+	u32 pcr;
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = a733_pwm_read_cfg(pc, ch, &cfg);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	rate = a733_pwm_src_rate(pc, &cfg);
+	if (!rate)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
+	if (readl(pc->base + A733_PWM_PCGR) & A733_PWM_PCGR_BYPASS(ch))
+		dev_warn_once(pwmchip_parent(chip),
+			      "ch%u: clock bypass is set, reported state ignores it\n",
+			      ch);
+
+	pcr = readl(pc->base + A733_PWM_PCR(ch));
+
+	state->period = a733_pwm_cycles_to_ns(rate, &cfg, cfg.entire);
+	state->duty_cycle = a733_pwm_cycles_to_ns(rate, &cfg, cfg.act);
+	state->polarity = (pcr & A733_PWM_PCR_ACT_STA) ? PWM_POLARITY_NORMAL
+						      : PWM_POLARITY_INVERSED;
+	state->enabled = a733_pwm_is_running(pc, ch);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static const struct pwm_ops a733_pwm_ops = {
+	.apply = a733_pwm_apply,
+	.get_state = a733_pwm_get_state,
+};
+
+static int a733_pwm_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
+{
+	struct device *dev = &pdev->dev;
+	struct reset_control *rst;
+	struct pwm_chip *chip;
+	struct a733_pwm *pc;
+	struct clk *bus;
+	unsigned int i;
+	int ret;
+
+	chip = devm_pwmchip_alloc(dev, A733_PWM_NPWM, sizeof(*pc));
+	if (IS_ERR(chip))
+		return PTR_ERR(chip);
+	pc = to_a733_pwm(chip);
+
+	pc->base = devm_platform_ioremap_resource(pdev, 0);
+	if (IS_ERR(pc->base))
+		return PTR_ERR(pc->base);
+
+	/*
+	 * "bus" is PWMn_BGR bit 0. Its CCU reset default is 0 = masked, so
+	 * without it every register below reads back as nothing at all.
+	 */
+	bus = devm_clk_get_enabled(dev, "bus");
+	if (IS_ERR(bus))
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(bus), "no bus clock\n");
+
+	for (i = 0; i < A733_PWM_SRC_NUM; i++) {
+		pc->src[i] = devm_clk_get_enabled(dev, a733_pwm_clk_names[i]);
+		if (IS_ERR(pc->src[i]))
+			return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(pc->src[i]),
+					     "no %s clock\n",
+					     a733_pwm_clk_names[i]);
+	}
+
+	/* PWMn_BGR bit 16, reset default 0 = asserted. */
+	rst = devm_reset_control_get_exclusive_deasserted(dev, NULL);
+	if (IS_ERR(rst))
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(rst), "cannot deassert reset\n");
+
+	chip->ops = &a733_pwm_ops;
+
+	/*
+	 * Nothing is enabled, gated on or written here on purpose. PER, PCGR
+	 * and every PCR come out of reset as 0 (UM 18.7.8), so a controller
+	 * that nobody has requested a channel from leaves its pins alone -- and
+	 * a board that muxes a PWM pin to something else is not disturbed by
+	 * this driver having probed.
+	 */
+	ret = devm_pwmchip_add(dev, chip);
+	if (ret)
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "cannot register PWM chip\n");
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static const struct of_device_id a733_pwm_dt_ids[] = {
+	{ .compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-pwm" },
+	{ /* sentinel */ }
+};
+MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(of, a733_pwm_dt_ids);
+
+static struct platform_driver a733_pwm_driver = {
+	.driver = {
+		.name = "sun60i-a733-pwm",
+		.of_match_table = a733_pwm_dt_ids,
+	},
+	.probe = a733_pwm_probe,
+};
+module_platform_driver(a733_pwm_driver);
+
+MODULE_DESCRIPTION("Allwinner A733 (sun60iw2) PWM driver");
+MODULE_LICENSE("GPL");
